{
  "level": "c1",
  "target": "en",
  "grammarSchedule": [
    {
      "id": "en.c1.conditional-mixed-modal-regret",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "If + past perfect is B1 (`en.b1.conditional-third`) and might have/could have are A2/B1 modal-perfects, so `If she had left earlier, she might have caught the train` needs no C1 word at all - only the new combination of a modal other than would."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.conditional-formal-inversion",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Had I known/Were I you/Should you need use only earlier grammar (past perfect, be, should); it reads as formal from the inversion alone, and lesson 1's connectors of formal writing (thus, hitherto, henceforth, albeit) already set the register it belongs in."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.conditional-elliptical-if-clause",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "If + adjective/past participle (if necessary, if elected) drops a subject and verb that could be any earlier-level pair; nothing in the C1 word list is required to demonstrate the ellipsis."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.passive-nonfinite-summarizing",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Having been warned/told/asked and it is believed that... both need only a transitive verb already available from B1/B2 (warn, tell, believe); a C1 word adds color but is never the blocker."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.future-perfect-nuance",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Will have + past participle is B2, and probably/obviously are earlier-level stance adverbs, so `You will probably have heard the news by now` is writable on day one of C1."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.future-continuous-tentative",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Might/may + be + -ing needs only the A2/B1 modals already tagged freely; the tentativeness is a function of the modal, not of any new noun or verb."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.modal-hedging-emphasis",
      "fromLesson": 4,
      "why": "Well, really and possibly are all pre-C1 adverbs and would already work at lesson 1, but lesson 4 (hedging an adverb) hands over predominantly and invariably in the same hedging/emphasis family, so from here a sentence can draw on either set for a more idiomatic C1 pairing."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.modal-past-nuance",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Might have/could have + past participle only reuses earlier modal-perfect forms; what is new is the pragmatic function (consoling, justifying, criticizing), which any everyday past event can carry from lesson 1."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.inversion-formal-focus",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Only when, little and such are all earlier-level (little at A1/A2, such at B1), so the inversion itself is the only new material, and it fits the formal register lesson 1 already opens with."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.cleft-wh-what",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "A what-clause as subject needs only what (A1) plus any clause; the focus construction is purely structural."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.nominalisation-formality",
      "fromLesson": 19,
      "why": "By lesson 19 (the nouns of an argument (6): endorsement, compliance) the course has already taught the matching verbs - to endorse in lesson 8 and to comply in lesson 10 - so a sentence can enact the verb-to-noun shift directly (he endorsed the plan -> his endorsement of the plan) instead of just using an abstract noun in isolation."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.non-finite-clauses-focus",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Faced with... and if elected... need only a past participle and the conditional grammar already in place from B1; no C1 vocabulary gates it."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.negation-formal-emphatic",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Not all/not every reuses A1 not/all/every, and a stance adverb after not (not surprisingly) reuses an earlier-level adverb; the bank has no word for whatsoever, so that one sub-pattern is not available, but the other two are from lesson 1."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.discourse-markers-textual-reference",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "As mentioned above needs only above (A1) and to mention (B1). The bank has no separate word for the latter, and former is banked only in its B1 sense of ehemalig/previous, not the this-one-not-that-one sense the topic wants - so sentences should lean on as mentioned above rather than the former/the latter."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.sense-verb-object-infinitive",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "I saw him leave needs only see and leave, both pre-C1; the bare-infinitive-vs-ing contrast is grammatical, not lexical."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.quantifier-either-neither-hyperbole",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Either/neither are already B2 vocabulary (free to tag), and a million is A1, so I've told you a million times is writable immediately."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.past-simple-emphatic-did",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Emphatic did just stresses an ordinary past-simple verb already known from A1/A2; no new word is involved."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.present-tense-rhetorical-persuasive",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "A rhetorical or persuasive present-tense question reuses ordinary present-simple question grammar from A1/A2; the persuasive force comes from context, not vocabulary."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.superlative-postmodifier",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "A superlative followed by a relative clause (the best film I've ever seen) already works with B1 grammar. The C1 word available (lesson 81, everyday judgment (1)) later gives an even more natural postmodifier (the best solution available), but the topic does not have to wait for it."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.would-adverb-stance",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Probably, certainly and obviously are all pre-C1 adverbs, so would probably/certainly/obviously is available as soon as would itself (B1) is in use."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.article-preposition-formal-register",
      "fromLesson": 3,
      "why": "The bank has no on account of or in accordance with, but lesson 3 (fixed phrases) supplies at random and in vain, genuinely article-less fixed prepositional phrases (contrast the everyday in a random way), which is where this topic finds real material in this bank."
    },
    {
      "id": "en.c1.exclamation-formal-pointing",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "Herein is not in the bank, so the herein lies the problem pattern is out of reach; the same fronting-for-focus effect is available from lesson 1 with such (B1), as in Such was the confusion that no one spoke."
    }
  ],
